        "synthesis": {
          "model_calls": 1,
          "phase": "synthesis",
          "plan": {
            "A": "Start from the fastest admissible C program (6d28bdba). Pursue a correctness-preserving low-overhead refinement of its dense/in-place segmented-prefix DP: assess OpenMP team/barrier and metadata costs, thresholds, chunk sizing, and compact blocked storage. Retain only independently measured improvements over 0.02249 s; do not assume a merge is beneficial.",
            "B": "Investigate a robust hybrid algorithm centered on the fast sparse row-major representation from 4abe95d54364e9faf388302e32ca7b4aab7e04f0. Compare sorted sparse obstacles/row spans against dense byte-grid/in-place DP and, only if fully scalable for declared inputs, combinatorial obstacle DP. Focus on duplicate obstacles, blocked endpoints, modular arithmetic, memory limits, and measured crossover selection.",
            "C": "Independently seek the best ARM64 four-core implementation of the row recurrence. Explore safe segmented scans versus sequential vector-friendly scans, obstacle encoding/layout, fast input, and branch behavior. Use the 6d28bdba implementation as the performance reference, validate all edge cases, and report measured candidates rather than speculative parallelism.",
            "G": "Integrate by benchmarking whole admissible programs and any compatible components; begin with 6d28bdba (0.02249 s) and 4abe95d (0.02303 s). Do not combine changes merely because they help separately. Select the fastest validated complete solution or a measured hybrid, preserving arbitrary valid-input correctness and avoiding source/test/budget changes.",
            "analysis": "The official task is Problem D: count monotone paths modulo 1e9+7 on an N×N grid with blocked cells. Baseline is slow mainly from dense vector<bool> storage, two DP rows, copy-back, iostream, and modulo remainder (0.08104 s). The strongest measured program is C's in-place row DP with a correct per-row segmented parallel scan, 0.02249 s; A's sorted sparse row-major spans plus buffered input is nearly tied at 0.02303 s. Dense byte-grid versions are materially slower (~0.0488 s). B's combinatorial inclusion-exclusion candidates are admissible and near 0.0245 s on this run but have quadratic obstacle dependence, so require careful scalability/crossover scrutiny. Assign complementary work: A tunes the current winner, B evaluates representation/algorithm regimes, C investigates independent ARM64 scan engineering, and G performs empirical non-additive integration."
          },
